<WorkPage
  title="Kubernetes RBAC Operator"
  oneLiner="A custom Kubernetes operator that turned authorization provisioning from a two-hour manual process into declarative configuration that maintains itself."
  role="Sole designer and implementer"
  timeframe="FICO Platform"
  stack={['Java Operator SDK', 'Kubernetes', 'gRPC']}
  metrics={[
    { value: '2h → ~8m', label: 'provisioning time' },
    { value: '20+', label: 'services provisioned' },
    { value: '4', label: 'custom resource types' },
    { value: '5 min', label: 'reconcile interval' },
  ]}
  description="A custom Kubernetes operator (Java Operator SDK) that reconciles declarative RBAC configuration, cutting provisioning from 2 hours to about 8 minutes."
>
  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    Every service on the platform needs authorization data before it can serve its
    first request: which roles exist, which permissions attach to which resources,
    which groups grant which roles. Defining that data was manual. Engineers drove
    a gRPC authoring API by hand, call by call, for every role, permission, and
    mapping their service needed. A typical change took about two hours, and a
    mistake did not show up until runtime, as a service denying legitimate access
    or granting access it should not have.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then the platform moved to a cell-based architecture, where teams deploy into
    multiple isolated environments. Authorization data had to be defined
    separately in every cell a team deployed to, so the manual work stopped
    growing linearly and started growing per team, per service, per cell. The
    provisioning process that was merely tedious for one environment became a
    real bottleneck for onboarding.
  </p>

  <h2>What I built</h2>
  <p>
    I designed and built the operator end to end: the custom resource schemas, the
    reconcile strategy, and the integration with the authoring API. For scope
    honesty, the authoring API itself and the platform's wider policy
    distribution architecture were team-level designs that I worked within; the
    operator is the piece where every design decision was mine.
  </p>
  <p>
    The operator defines four custom resource types, each covering one area of
    authorization data: capabilities (a service that owns protectable resources),
    custom roles, resource-to-permission mappings, and role-to-group bootstrap
    mappings. Teams fill in templated YAML, check it into git, and deploy the
    resources alongside their services. From that point the operator owns keeping
    reality in sync with what the YAML declares.
  </p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>
    An operator is a control loop, not a script. It never runs once and exits; it
    continuously compares the state teams have declared against the state that
    actually exists, and corrects the difference. I have written a longer
    explainer on the pattern (<a href="/writing/what-a-kubernetes-operator-actually-is/"
    >what a Kubernetes operator actually is</a>), but the short version is a
    thermostat: you declare the temperature you want, and the system continuously
    works out how to get there.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <div class="diagram">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 740 320" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: YAML custom resources flow from git through the Kubernetes API server to the operator, which reconciles them into the policy authoring API and authorization store">
        <defs>
          <marker id="arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
