  <p>
    The flow: a team applies its custom resources, and admission webhooks validate
    the group-to-role, permission-to-role, and permission-to-resource mappings at
    deploy time, so a malformed configuration is rejected in seconds rather than
    discovered at runtime. The operator watches those resources and also resyncs
    on a five-minute interval. On each reconcile it reads what the resource
    declares, compares it to what the authoring API reports as live, and writes
    only when something actually differs. Status conditions on each resource
    surface the outcome, so a team can check provisioning state with
    <code>kubectl</code> instead of filing a ticket. I also added throttling so a
    burst of changes does not hammer the authoring API.
  </p>
  <p>
    The interval matters as much as the watch. Because reconciliation is
    level-triggered rather than edge-triggered, the operator picks up resources
    created while it was down, retries transient failures on the next cycle, and
    corrects drift: if the live data no longer matches what a resource declares,
    the next reconcile puts it back. That is what makes the system self-healing
    rather than merely automated.
  </p>

  <h2>Trade-offs</h2>

  <Tradeoff decision="Declarative custom resources over the imperative authoring API as the provisioning path">
    <span slot="bought">
      GitOps for authorization: config is version-controlled, reviewable, and
      rolled back like code. Schema validation happens at deploy time, and one
      YAML template serves every cell a team deploys to.
    </span>
    <span slot="cost">
      State now has two entry points. Data declared through a custom resource
      self-heals, but data created directly through the API with no owning
      resource is invisible to the operator, and nothing reconciles it. Drift
      detection for that ungoverned data is the first thing I would add.
    </span>
  </Tradeoff>

  <Tradeoff decision="A continuous reconcile loop over one-shot provisioning scripts">
    <span slot="bought">
      Self-healing by construction: drift correction, crash recovery, and retry
      of transient failures all fall out of the loop for free, with no special
      error-handling paths.
    </span>
    <span slot="cost">
      Convergence is eventual, not immediate. An out-of-band change can persist
      for up to one reconcile interval before it is corrected, and every cycle
      spends reads against the authoring API even when nothing changed.
    </span>
  </Tradeoff>

  <Tradeoff decision="Validation at admission time over validation inside the reconcile loop">
    <span slot="bought">
      Bad configuration is rejected before it is ever stored. The team gets
      feedback at <code>kubectl apply</code>, in seconds, with a message naming
      the invalid mapping.
    </span>
    <span slot="cost">
      The webhook sits on the API server's request path for these resource
      types, so it has to stay fast and highly available; a slow validator would
      degrade every deploy that touches authorization config.
    </span>
  </Tradeoff>

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      RBAC provisioning went from about 2 hours of manual API work to roughly 8
      minutes of writing YAML, across 20+ microservices.
    </li>
    <li>
      Misconfigurations are caught at deploy time by schema validation and
      admission webhooks, before they can affect a running service.
    </li>
    <li>
      Teams onboard once per use case instead of once per cell: the same
      templated resources deploy to every environment, and the operator applies
      and maintains them everywhere.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What I'd do differently</h2>
  <p>
    The operator only governs data that has an owning custom resource, and I
    would close that gap first: a detection pass that flags store data with no
    corresponding resource, so the declarative path is the only unaudited-change
    path. I would also instrument the loop itself from day one, reconcile
    latency, writes per cycle, and time-to-convergence after a change, because a
    control loop you cannot observe is a control loop you cannot debug.
  </p>

  <p class="meta">
